  "slug": "crispri-responses-25da",
  "summary": "Two CRISPRi preprints show knockdown responses and rejuvenation rankings depend on cell line and cohort, limiting naive transfer of perturbation results.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how far CRISPRi perturbation responses generalize beyond the context in which they were measured. CRISPRi Perturb-seq of essential genes in four human cell lines finds a median cross-line response correlation of 0.52. Transfer to a new line requires screening about 30% of it. A separate analysis of CRISPRi screens ranked for reversal of aging expression signatures finds that the rankings change with the aging cohort used and track proliferation programs, pointing to growth-related confounding. The previously noted comparison of drug-perturbation resources is not among the current items, so that claim is dropped. Adjacent methods address technical confounding and prediction rather than CRISPRi itself. These include cross-modal anchored batch correction for TCGA multi-omics, DeepRNAi for siRNA on- and off-target prediction, ARC-Impute and spicyR v2 for spatial omics, and assay-level comparisons covering Siphon-seq platelet profiling, Catch*ReSeq isoform enrichment and cell-free RNA extraction platforms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
